<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bahram Chobin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 591</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Bahram VI</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bahram Chobin was a Sasanian general of the Mihran family who defeated the Turks in 588–589. Dismissed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormizd-iv">Hormizd IV</a> after a setback against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, he rebelled and, after Hormizd's overthrow, drove the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> from the throne, ruling as Bahram VI in 590–591. Khosrow returned with help from the emperor Maurice and defeated him; Bahram fled to the Turks and was murdered. He became a hero of Persian romance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bahram-chobin.jpg" alt="Bahram Chobin" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BahramChobinCoinHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bahram was a member of the House of Mihran, one of the seven Great Houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, a family of Parthian origin centred on Ray. His father, Bahram Gushnasp, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>, and his grandfather Gurgin Milad was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marzban">marzban</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> from 572 to 574. His nickname Chobin, 'wooden shaft' or 'javelin-like', referred to his tall, slender build; his epithet Mihrbandak means servant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a>. Parvaneh Pourshariati argues that he was probably a worshipper of Mithra, since both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a>'s Shahnameh and Sebeos record him as one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bahram began as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marzban">marzban</a> of Ray, took part in the capture of the Byzantine stronghold of Dara, and became army chief (spahbed) of the north. In 588 the Turkic khagan Bagha Qaghan and his Hephthalite subjects invaded the Sasanian lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, Talaqan, Badghis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. Bahram, given the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and a reported 12,000 hand-picked horsemen, ambushed them at Hyrcanian rock in April 588 and again in 589, retook Balkh, crossed the Oxus and personally killed Bagha Qaghan with an arrow. His victory reached as far as Baykand, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>.</p><p>The shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormizd-iv">Hormizd IV</a>, already distrustful, removed him from office and humiliated him; sources differ on the reason, and the Sasanian court was suspicious of his rising fame. Bahram rebelled, the first time a Parthian dynast had challenged the Sasanian family's legitimacy. Before he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, Hormizd was blinded and killed in a palace coup that put his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> on the throne. Bahram defeated Khosrow's men at the Nahrawan Canal and took Ctesiphon, and in the summer of 590 declared himself king of kings, claiming to restore the Arsacids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Many hailed Bahram as the promised saviour of Zoroastrian apocalyptic belief, but nobles and priests largely sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>, who gained Byzantine help from the emperor Maurice. In 591 Khosrow and the Byzantine armies invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and Bahram was defeated at the Battle of Blarathon. He fled east with 4,000 men to the Turkic khagan, probably Birmudha, whom he had once captured, and served him until Khosrow had him assassinated through bribes to the Turkic royal family.</p><p>His son Noshrad was the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, who stressed their descent from him. Bahram's life is told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> romance Bahrām Chōbīn Nāma, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> chapters on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormizd-iv">Hormizd IV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> are almost as much about him. Among Iranian nationalists he remained popular after the Arab conquest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bahram Chobin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The immediate trigger was the Byzantine-Sasanian War of 602–628 CE, launched after the emperor Maurice, who had helped restore Khosrow II to power against the usurper <b>Bahram Chobin</b> in 591 CE, was overthrown and murdered by the junior officer Phocas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
